 (report . "APPROVED. Round 8 (independent, read-only VERIFY subagent) certified the repaired v1.00.30 W4 wave at implementation head 391b8d29df485d65cf7af6031b153df5aae2ec60 with content digest c37cb466fcb76ac399c2145201f6f857f3c55ea901a652fe5b532e6dd605f83b, with no blocking findings. The round-7 contradiction is resolved in the retained evidence: artifacts/ci-recovery/v1.00.30-w4/raw/verify-chain.txt:230-231 carries only the two benign canonical-form notes for this artifact dir and no SHA256SUMS drift note; the capture is green (focused 184 tests, arch/security/workflows/deps/metrics/provenance exits 0, fast 1208/1208); sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS passes for all six bound files. The report labels ba35b090f as the Repaired code / drill head and distinguishes the schema-2 implementation head, both ancestors carrying the recorded content, and the drill transcript, the structured eager-fallback-ms block and the report prose all agree on 472/481/480 ms. The reviewer confirmed the full acceptance surface: the bounded eager gate with the restore record and upload ordered after it, the two-axis telemetry classification with unobserved costs omitted, the launcher usable-root telemetry uploaded after the shard, fast-pilot-only activation with no broadening, auto handled in both CLIs with every other value failing closed, the trusted-root excluded from checkout materialization while its path stays exposed, the W2 checkpoint pin cascade, and the required-check schema-2 identity. No regression against main's v1.00.31 W1 invocation and per-module build/run contract was found. Rounds 1-7 findings (producer input, zero-module run, linear history, W2 hygiene, lane wiring, off-lane eager gate and honest W4 telemetry, stale artifact heads, record-before-gate ordering, drill-timing single-sourcing, and the SHA256SUMS-consistent capture) are all fixed and recorded in the red-first captures and the repair narrative. The wave is approved for merge through the protected gate with the operator merge authorization bound to this exact head. The implementation PR's CI then surfaced two latent post-squash defects in already-published state, both repaired at 391b8d29df485d65cf7af6031b153df5aae2ec60: the W6 rehearsal matrix recorded a pre-squash W6 branch tip as its rehearsal head, which became unfetchable when the branch was deleted at wave close (re-stamped to the published W6 binding commit fc1a6fa36 with byte-identical rehearsal inputs, generator rule documented, workflows job on fetch-depth 0), and the round-6 eager-gate timing rewrite tripped the W1 invocation contract's not-a-script guard (gate restored to a single-line raco make main.rkt with the cost bracketed by stamp steps). Round 9 (focused delta review) then found that the round-8 fix had also edited the matrix generator, which is itself a recorded rehearsal input, so its recorded input digest no longer matched the committed bytes; the generator was restored byte-identical to its W6 form (the recorded-head operational rule lives in the rehearsal report, which is SHA256SUMS-bound and not an inputs-digest entry), SHA256SUMS was rebound over the changed report, and every recorded rehearsal-input digest is truthful again. The final delta (generator restore, rebound sums, refreshed chain capture) is committed at 391b8d29df485d65cf7af6031b153df5aae2ec60 and a final focused delta review is solicited against it; this entry carries that verdict (and its narrative) before the implementation pull request is merged."))